\begin_body

\begin_layout Section*
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Thorson's
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 epsilon correction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The core of the REML/ML methods implemented in 
\family typewriter
mgcv
\family default
 is Laplace-approximate maximum likelihood (LAML).
 In the case where Laplace approximations are used,
 we can use the correction 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

proposed
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "thorson_implementing_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and elaborated on in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "thorson_spatiotemporal_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that there are a couple of mathematical errors in both of these sources.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Scare quotes are used here as the correction is based on 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tierney_fully_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 from their Theorem 2.
 They propose estimators for the expectation and variance of some function 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 of the model parameters (
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

) when a model is estimated using a Laplace approximation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can generally write the expectation as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\left[g(\theta)\right]=\frac{\int g(\theta)L(\theta)\pi(\theta)d\theta}{\int L(\theta)\pi(\theta)d\theta}.\label{eq:expect1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

As usual 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the likelihood and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the prior on parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 We can write 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:expect1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 in the following form (equation 2.2 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tierney_fully_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\left[g(\theta)\right]=\frac{\int b_{N}(\theta)\exp\left[-nh_{N}(\theta)\right]d\theta}{\int b_{D}(\theta)\exp\left[-nh_{D}(\theta)\right]d\theta},\label{eq:expect1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 denote the numerator and denominator versions of the functions,
 respectively.
 We can see that our problem fits into the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

standard form
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{N}(\theta)=g(\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{D}(\theta)=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{N}(\theta)=h_{D}(\theta)=-(1/n)\log_{e}\left[L(\theta)\pi(\theta)\right]$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "tierney_accurate_1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 however 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tierney_fully_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 also introduce the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exponential form
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which we will use below.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Analysis proceeds by looking at 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left\{ \exp[sg(\theta)]\right\} $
\end_inset

 (the moment generating function).
 Plugging these values in and taking derivatives will yield Theorem 2 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tierney_fully_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (see paper for fine details on this):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\left[g\left(\hat{\theta}\right)\right] & =g\left(\hat{\theta}\right)+\frac{d}{ds}\log_{e}\sigma_{s}\vert_{s=0}+\frac{d}{ds}\log_{e}b(\theta_{s})\vert_{s=0}\quad\text{and}\nonumber \\
\hat{\text{Var}}\left[g\left(\hat{\theta}\right)\right] & =\left[\sigma g^{\prime}\left(\hat{\theta}\right)\right]^{2}/n+\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\log_{e}\sigma_{s}\vert_{s=0}+\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\log_{e}b(\theta_{s})\vert_{s=0}.\label{eq:theorem2}
\end{align}

\end_inset

This is equivalent to expressions 2e and 2f in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "thorson_implementing_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and equation 6.8 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "thorson_spatiotemporal_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Note that this is only for the case where we have parameter,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

,
 to estimate.
 In the case of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tierney_fully_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "thorson_implementing_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "thorson_spatiotemporal_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 the multivariate case is alluded to,
 but not fully expanded.
 The rest of the appendix derives this for the specific log-normal case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Extending to the multivariate case
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the above,
 we are only looking at univariate 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

,
 we need to extend to the multivariate case.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tierney_fully_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 state (in the proof of Theorem 2):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\shape italic
In the multivariate statement of the theorem,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{s}$
\end_inset

 would be replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $\det\left(\Sigma_{s}\right)^{1/2}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{s}=\left[D^{2}h_{s}\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\sigma g^{\prime}(\theta)\right]^{2}$
\end_inset

 would be replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $(Dg)^{T}\Sigma(Dg)$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $Dg$
\end_inset

 is the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 [with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\theta}$
\end_inset

] evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma=\left[D^{2}h\vert_{\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}}\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\shape default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that here 
\begin_inset Formula $nh_{s}(\boldsymbol{\theta})=nh(\boldsymbol{\theta})-sg(\boldsymbol{\theta})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Doing the suggested substitution and letting 
\begin_inset Formula $b(\boldsymbol{\theta})=1$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:theorem2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\left[g\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\right)\right] & =g\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\right)+\frac{d}{ds}\log_{e}\det\left(\Sigma_{s}\right)^{1/2}\vert_{s=0}\quad\text{and}\nonumber \\
\hat{\text{Var}}\left[g\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\right)\right] & =(Dg)^{T}\Sigma(Dg)/n+\left.\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\log_{e}\det\left(\Sigma_{s}\right)^{1/2}\right|_{s=0}.\label{eq:theorem2-1}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Note that the last term in each equation evaluates to zero since 
\begin_inset Formula $b(\boldsymbol{\theta})=1$
\end_inset

 (per above) and hence has no derivatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Finding 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find quantities such as 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d}{ds}h(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})$
\end_inset

 we need to find 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}$
\end_inset

 (since the resulting derivative is 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{\prime}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}$
\end_inset

,
 by the chain rule).
 We will generally evaluate that expression at 
\begin_inset Formula $s=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By analogy to equation 3.8 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tierney_fully_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we find
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
0 & =\frac{\partial f}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s},s}=\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}\left[nh(\boldsymbol{\theta})-sg(\boldsymbol{\theta})\right]\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s},s}\\
 & =n\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})-s\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}).
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Then taking derivatives wrt 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $s=0$
\end_inset

,
 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
0 & =\frac{d}{ds}\left[n\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})-s\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\right]\vert_{s=0}\\
 & =nD^{2}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\\
\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0} & =\frac{1}{n}\left[D^{2}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right]^{-1}\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\\
 & =\frac{1}{n}\Sigma\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\\
 & =\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Finding 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d^{2}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds^{2}}\vert_{s=0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finding the second derivatives we use the same setup and evaluate
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
0 & =\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\left[n\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}h(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})-s\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\right]\vert_{s=0}\\
 & =\frac{d}{ds}\left[nD^{2}h(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}-\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})-sD^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\right]\vert_{s=0}\\
 & =n\frac{d}{ds}D^{2}h(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-\frac{d}{ds}\frac{\partial}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\vert_{s=0}-\frac{d}{ds}\left(sD^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\right)\vert_{s=0}\\
 & =n\frac{d}{ds}D^{2}h(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-\left(D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}+\cancelto{0}{s\frac{d}{ds}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}}\right)\\
 & =n\left(\frac{dD^{2}h(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}+D^{2}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d}{ds}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\right)-D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\\
 & =nD^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}+nD^{2}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d^{2}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds^{2}}\vert_{s=0}-2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\\
-nD^{2}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d^{2}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds^{2}}\vert_{s=0} & =nD^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\\
D^{2}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d^{2}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds^{2}}\vert_{s=0} & =\frac{1}{n}\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-nD^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\right)\\
\frac{d^{2}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds^{2}}\vert_{s=0} & =\frac{1}{n}D^{2}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})^{-1}\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-nD^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{n}\Sigma\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-nD^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Finally substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}=\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})$
\end_inset

 yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{d^{2}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds^{2}}\vert_{s=0} & =\frac{1}{n}\Sigma\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}-nD^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{n}\Sigma\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})-nD^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{n^{2}}\Sigma\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})-D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since
\begin_inset Formula $D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})$
\end_inset

 is a 3-tensor (3 dimensional array),
 we note explicitly that 
\begin_inset Formula $D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}\vert_{s=0}$
\end_inset

 has the contraction 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})=(\left[D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right]\left[\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right]_{k}\left[\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right]_{l}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Finding the derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $\log_{e}\det\left(\Sigma_{s}\right)^{1/2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First derivatives are needed for the expectation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{ds}\log_{e}\left[\det\left(\Sigma_{s}\right)^{1/2}\right]\vert_{s=0} & =\frac{\frac{d}{ds}\det\left(\Sigma_{s}\right)^{1/2}\vert_{s=0}}{\det\left(\Sigma\right)^{1/2}}\\
 & =\frac{1/2\det\left(\Sigma\right)^{-1/2}\frac{d}{ds}\det\Sigma_{s}\vert_{s=0}}{\det\left(\Sigma\right)^{1/2}}\\
 & =1/2\det\left(\Sigma_{}\right)^{-1}\frac{d}{ds}\det\Sigma_{s}\vert_{s=0}\\
 & =1/2\det\left(\Sigma\right)^{-1}\det\Sigma_{}\text{trace}\left(\Sigma^{-1}\frac{d}{ds}\Sigma_{s}\vert_{s=0}\right)\\
 & =1/2\text{trace}\left(\Sigma^{-1}\frac{d}{ds}\Sigma_{s}\vert_{s=0}\right)\\
 & =1/2\text{trace}\left(\Sigma^{-1}\frac{d}{ds}\left[D^{2}h_{s}\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right]^{-1}\vert_{s=0}\right)\\
 & =-1/2\text{trace}\left(\Sigma^{-1}\left[D^{2}h_{s}\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right]^{-1}\frac{d}{ds}\left[D^{2}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}-\frac{s}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right]\vert_{s=0}\left[D^{2}h_{s}\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right]^{-1}\right)\\
 & =-1/2\text{trace}\left(\Sigma^{-1}\Sigma\frac{d}{ds}\left[D^{2}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}-\frac{s}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right]\vert_{s=0}\Sigma\right)\\
 & =-1/2\text{trace}\left(\frac{d}{ds}\left[D^{2}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}-\frac{s}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right]\vert_{s=0}\Sigma\right)\\
 & =-1/2\text{trace}\left(\left[D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})\frac{d\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}{ds}-\frac{d}{ds}\left(\frac{s}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right)\vert_{s=0}\right]\Sigma\right)\\
 & =-1/2\text{trace}\left(\left[D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})\frac{1}{n}Dg(\boldsymbol{\theta})\Sigma-\frac{d}{ds}\left(\frac{s}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right)\vert_{s=0}\right]\Sigma\right)\\
 & =-1/2\text{trace}\left(\left[D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})\frac{1}{n}Dg(\boldsymbol{\theta})\Sigma-\left(\left(\frac{d}{ds}\frac{s}{n}\vert_{s=0}\right)D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}+\frac{s}{n}\left(\frac{d}{ds}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{s=0}\right)\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right)\right]\Sigma\right)\\
 & =-1/2\text{trace}\left(\left[D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})\frac{1}{n}Dg(\boldsymbol{\theta})\Sigma-\left(\left(\frac{d}{ds}\frac{s}{n}\vert_{s=0}\right)D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right)\right]\Sigma\right)\\
 & =-1/2\text{trace}\left(\left[\frac{1}{n}D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})Dg(\boldsymbol{\theta})\Sigma-\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\right]\Sigma\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{2n}\text{trace}\left(D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\Sigma-D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})[\Sigma,\Sigma Dg]\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

This is analogous to the expression given in equation 6.8 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "thorson_spatiotemporal_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and the final expression in section 3.1 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tierney_fully_1989"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We note that 
\begin_inset Formula $D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})$
\end_inset

 forms a 3D tensor (a 3 dimensional array of derivatives).
 As such,
 we use the contraction such that 
\begin_inset Formula $D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})[\Sigma,\Sigma Dg]=T_{jkl}\Sigma_{jk}(\Sigma Dg)_{l}$
\end_inset

 where subscripts on the right hand side indicate the indices in the three dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $j,k,l$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\boldsymbol{\theta})$
\end_inset

 were just a normal distribution this term would be zero,
 as the PDF of a normal distribution does not have third derivatives.
 However,
 here we have a log-normal distribution so those derivatives do exist.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the variance expression,
 we need to also evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\log_{e}\det\left(\Sigma_{s}\right)^{1/2}$
\end_inset

.
 We begin by writing:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A(s)=D^{2}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})−\frac{s}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s}),
\]

\end_inset

note that then 
\begin_inset Formula $Σ_{s}=A(s)^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Taking the first derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $A(s)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 we obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{dA(s)}{ds} & =\frac{d}{ds}D^{2}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})−\frac{d}{ds}\frac{s}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\\
 & =D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{θ}_{s}}{ds}−\frac{d}{ds}\frac{s}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\\
 & =D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{θ}_{s}}{ds}−\left(\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})+\frac{s}{n}\frac{d}{ds}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\right)\\
 & =D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{θ}_{s}}{ds}−\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})-\frac{s}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\frac{d\boldsymbol{θ}_{s}}{ds}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

And now the second derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $A(s)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and letting 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}=\frac{d\boldsymbol{θ}_{s}}{ds}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime\prime}=\frac{d^{2}\boldsymbol{θ}_{s}}{ds^{2}}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{d^{2}A(s)}{ds^{2}} & =\frac{d}{ds}\left[D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}−\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})-\frac{s}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\right]\\
 & =\frac{d}{ds}\left(D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\right)−\frac{d}{ds}\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})-\frac{d}{ds}\frac{s}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\\
 & =D^{4}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}+D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})D^{2}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}−\frac{1}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})D\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}-\left[\frac{1}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})D\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}+\frac{s}{n}\frac{d}{ds}\left(D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})D\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}\right)\right]\\
 & =D^{4}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}+D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime\prime}−\frac{1}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}-\left[\frac{1}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}+\frac{s}{n}\left(\frac{d}{ds}\left[D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\right]\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}+D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\frac{d}{ds}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\right)\right]\\
 & =D^{4}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}+D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime\prime}−\frac{2}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}-\frac{s}{n}\left(\frac{d}{ds}\left[D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\right]\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}+D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\frac{d}{ds}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\right).
\end{align*}

\end_inset

We leave the last term with the leading 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 here as we are going to evaluate the expression at 
\begin_inset Formula $s=0$
\end_inset

 in a moment so that term will go to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now note that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\det\Sigma_{s}=-\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\det A(s),
\]

\end_inset

so by usual matrix identities
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{ds}\left[-\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\det A(s)\right] & =-\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(A(s)^{-1}\frac{dA(s)}{ds}\right)\;\;\text{and}\\
\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\det A(s)\right]\vert_{s=0} & =\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\left[\Sigma\frac{dA(s)}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\right]^{2}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\Sigma\frac{d^{2}A}{ds^{2}}\vert_{s=0}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Now taking these pieces:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\det\Sigma_{s} & =\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\det A(s)\right]\vert_{s=0}\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\left[\Sigma\frac{dA(s)}{ds}\vert_{s=0}\right]^{2}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\Sigma\frac{d^{2}A}{ds^{2}}\vert_{s=0}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\left[\Sigma\left(D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}−\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})-\cancelto{0}{\frac{s}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}}\right)\vert_{s=0}\right]^{2}\right)\\
 & \;\;-\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\Sigma\left(D^{4}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}+D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime\prime}−\frac{2}{n}D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}-\cancelto{0}{\frac{s}{n}\left(\frac{d}{ds}\left[D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\right]\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}+D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{θ}_{s})\frac{d}{ds}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\right)}\right)\vert_{s=0}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\left[\Sigma\left(D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}−\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\right)\right]^{2}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\Sigma\left(D^{4}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}+D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime\prime}\vert_{s=0}−\frac{2}{n}D^{3}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}\right)\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\left[\Sigma\left(D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}−\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\right)\right]^{2}\right)\\
 & \;\;-\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\Sigma\left(D^{4}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}+D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime\prime}\vert_{s=0}−\frac{2}{n}D^{3}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}\right)\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

and substituting the expressions for 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\frac{1}{2}\log_{e}\det\Sigma_{s} & =\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\left[\Sigma\left(D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}−\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\right)\right]^{2}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\Sigma\left(D^{4}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}+D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime\prime}\vert_{s=0}−\frac{2}{n}D^{3}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}^{\prime}\vert_{s=0}\right)\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\left[\Sigma\left(D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})−\frac{1}{n}D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\right)\right]^{2}\right)\\
 & \;\;-\frac{1}{2}\text{trace}\left(\Sigma\left(D^{4}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})+D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\left[\frac{1}{n^{2}}\Sigma\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})-D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)\right]−\frac{2}{n}D^{3}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\frac{1}{n}\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{2n^{2}}\text{trace}\Biggl(\left[\Sigma\left(D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})−D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\right)\right]^{2}\\
 & \;\;-\Sigma\left(D^{4}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})+D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\left[\Sigma\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})-D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)\right]−2D^{3}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)\Biggr)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Putting it together
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting the above into 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:theorem2-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 we then obtain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\left[g\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\right)\right] & =g\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\right)+\frac{1}{2n}\text{trace}\left(D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})\vert_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{s}}\Sigma-D^{3}h(\boldsymbol{\theta})[\Sigma,\Sigma Dg]\right)\quad\text{and}\nonumber \\
\hat{\text{Var}}\left[g\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\right)\right] & =(Dg)^{T}\Sigma(Dg)/n+\frac{1}{2n^{2}}\text{trace}\Biggl(\left[\Sigma\left(D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})−D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\right)\right]^{2}\nonumber \\
 & \;\;\;-\Sigma\left(D^{4}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})+D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\left[\Sigma\left(2D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})-D^{3}h(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)\right]−2D^{3}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{θ}})\Sigma Dg(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})\right)\Biggr).
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In practice,
 we can obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{V}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}=\Sigma/n$
\end_inset

 as the posterior covariance matrix (via 
\family typewriter
mgcv::vcov
\family default
,
 see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "wood_smoothing_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "miller_bayesian_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for more information on the meaning of these covariance matrices and how to include uncertainty from smoothing parameters).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $D^{2}g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}})=\left.\frac{\partial^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\theta})}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}}\right|_{\boldsymbol{\theta}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}}$
\end_inset

 (i.e.,
 the hessian of 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\boldsymbol{\theta})$
\end_inset

 at the REML-optimal parameters) by numerical derivatives (e.g.,
 
\family typewriter
numDeriv::hessian
\family default
;
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "paulgilbertandravivaradhan_numderiv_2006"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) or by analytical expression.
 Since the analytical expression is faster to compute (since we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\boldsymbol{\beta})=b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}$
\end_inset

),
 we calculate the 
\begin_inset Formula $j,k^{\text{th}}$
\end_inset

 entry in the hessian is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left[D^{2}g(\boldsymbol{\beta})\right]_{jk}=\frac{\partial^{2}b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}}{\partial\beta_{j}\partial\beta_{k}} & =\frac{\partial}{\partial\beta_{j}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\beta_{k}}b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}\\
 & =\frac{\partial}{\partial\beta_{j}}\left[\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial\beta_{k}}X\boldsymbol{\beta}\right)b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}\log_{e}b\right]\\
 & =\frac{\partial}{\partial\beta_{j}}\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{k}^{T}b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}\log_{e}b\right)\\
 & =\boldsymbol{x}_{k}^{T}\left(\log_{e}b\right)\frac{\partial}{\partial\beta_{j}}b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}\\
 & =\boldsymbol{x}_{k}^{T}\left(\log_{e}b\right)\boldsymbol{x}_{j}b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}\left(\log_{e}b\right)\\
 & =\left(\log_{e}b\right)^{2}\boldsymbol{x}_{k}^{T}\boldsymbol{x}_{j}b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is the base of the response (i.e.,
 we are modelling on the 
\begin_inset Formula $\log_{b}$
\end_inset

 scale) and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}_{k}$
\end_inset

 indicate a rows/columns of the design matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 By extension (and because we need it for the variance term):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left[D^{3}g(\boldsymbol{\beta})\right]_{ijk}=\frac{\partial^{3}b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}}}{\partial\beta_{j}\partial\beta_{k}\partial\beta_{l}} & =\left(\log_{e}b\right)^{3}\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{l}\times\boldsymbol{x}_{k}^{T}\boldsymbol{x}_{j}\right)b^{X\boldsymbol{\beta}},
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{l}\times\boldsymbol{x}_{k}^{T}\boldsymbol{x}_{j}\right)$
\end_inset

 is an order 3 tensor (i.e.,
 a 3-dimensional array).
\end_layout

